            <div class="text-slate-700 text-[16px] sm:text-[17px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
              <p>
                Travel times in guidebooks almost always assume you are staying in District 1. If your hotel is located elsewhere, adjust your morning schedule accordingly:
              </p>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700">
                <li><strong>Tan Binh / Phu Nhuan (Near Tan Son Nhat Airport):</strong> You are already on the northwestern side of town. You bypass central downtown traffic completely, shaving <strong>20 to 30 minutes</strong> off your outbound commute (for arrival and airport logistics, consult our <a href="/tan-son-nhat-airport-to-ben-thanh-transfer-guide" class="text-amber-800 hover:underline font-semibold">Tan Son Nhat Airport transfer guide</a>).</li>
                <li><strong>District 2 / Thao Dien / Thu Duc (East of the River):</strong> Your driver must either navigate through downtown rush hour or swing around the northern ring road. Add <strong>30 to 45 minutes</strong> to your one-way travel time.</li>
                <li><strong>District 7 / Phu My Hung (South of the City):</strong> You have to cross the entire length of urban Saigon from south to northwest. Expect your drive to take at least <strong>2 to 2.5 hours</strong> during morning commuting hours.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                If you book a private car, let your operator know your exact hotel address in advance so they can factor the cross-town drive into your morning pickup time.
              </p>
